<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dommoc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 630</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dommoc was the seat of the first bishopric of East Anglia, given around 630 by King Sigeberht to the Burgundian missionary Felix. It is traditionally identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunwich">Dunwich</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> coast, though some historians favour the Roman fort at Walton near Felixstowe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of Dommoc are recorded until the Danish conquest of East Anglia in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dommoc.jpg" alt="Dommoc" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:England_diocese_map_pre-925.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
